{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And My name HaShem.</b> Rashi explains that the Holy One, blessed be He, complains about the earlier ones — that He revealed Himself to them and they did not ask His name — whereas you already asked what His name is; what shall I say to them? And Rashi raises the difficulty that according to this, it should have written לא שאלו לי. And if you ask: did He not make known to them that such is His name? Behold, at the outset He revealed Himself etc. And on this the world is astonished: what is the explanation? And Rabbi Benjamin explains — I heard — that this is the meaning: and if you ask the verse that is written לא נודעתי, as though לא שאלו לי — it is impossible, for at the outset He revealed Himself etc., and by implication He made known to them that such is His name. If so, He was not complaining about the earlier ones according to the midrashic reading, but rather because they did not ask His name, whereas this one asked. If so, the difficulty returns to its place: it should have written לא שאלו לי."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The comment critiques Rashi's interpretation of Exodus 6:3. The phrase 'what shall I say to them' paraphrases Rashi's own gloss. The closing הדרא קושיא לדוכתא is a standard Talmudic idiom: 'the difficulty returns to its place.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:02:48.122291316Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}